title: Attachments: retracted
effort: S
area: format
created: 2026-09-17T04:28:34Z
updated: 2026-09-17T04:39:14Z

Retracted. An earlier revision made any file in a ticket directory an
attachment, on the grounds that a ticket is a directory and the affordance
was free. It was permission, not a mechanism, and for agents permission
without convention produces inconsistency.

Three reasons it was wrong. No naming convention, so a reading agent has to
ls and guess, which is the unbounded poking the format exists to prevent.
Nothing in info announces an attachment, so a bounded read misses it, and
the obvious fix — a field listing them — is derived data that can drift,
which desc: already is. And the location is wrong: an artifact worth keeping
is usually about the code, not the ticket, so it belongs next to the code
where everyone finds it and where closing the ticket does not archive it.

The need behind it was real. A subagent's output is ephemeral, and the
answer is that findings go in the body of the ticket it worked on, written
before the context is gone. desc: already bounds a long body, so it costs
only the agent that committed to the ticket. A standalone artifact goes in
the repo and the body references the path.

What survives is one rule, kept in 9 because the tree scan needs it
regardless: a directory inside a ticket that is neither a number nor c is
invalid, since the scan reads it as a phantom ticket.

This also raises the priority of 10 — writing findings into a body by hand
means fixing desc: by hand, which is the drift the format is worst at
detecting.
